{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792312279,"nonce":"yja4nR9uHxza1dgZ/7IgDRpKKG4=","title":"A page's frame is split by a feature query, and texture levels each send a whole buffer","message":"Two causes of a failed first frame in a page with 3D textures.\n\n**A frame sent in two**\n\nA page sends a frame's commands as one batch, and the swapchain texture it acquires lives only to the end of that task. `MeshTextures.get` asked the device `supports(TextureCompressionBc)` for each texture, and in a page that is a round trip, which flushes the batch in the middle of the frame: the next batch's submit then used an expired texture (\"Destroyed texture … used in a submit\"). The answer is now asked once, when the renderer is made.\n\nCompression jobs were also started from inside the frame; they now start after its submit and error check, so waking a thread cannot come between a frame's commands.\n\n**Whole buffers**\n\nThe uncompressed upload resampled each level into one scratch buffer the size of level 0 and wrote the whole buffer for every level, because a write sends all of the bytes it is given. For a 4096 by 4096 texture that is 13 writes of 64 MB, and two such textures took a page's memory from 757 MB to 1 GiB, where an allocation failed. Each level now gets a buffer of exactly its size; the peak went to 889 MB for the two full-size car textures.","files":null}]}